Whether an 8-bit quantized detector stays reliable under corruption cannot be read off the format label: it depends on how the engine was built and which model region is quantized. We pair every corrupted input with a codec-matched clean control, so identical bytes reach executable TensorRT INT8 and FP8 engines, and estimate the corruption-associated change in the FP8--INT8 average-precision (AP) gap, $\Delta E$, inside common-image bootstrap samples. Across nine dataset$\times$scale blocks of YOLO11 on KITTI, VOC, and a 2,000-image COCO subset, $\Delta E$ is strongly heterogeneous ($I^2=\nnHetIsq\%$) and both signs survive Holm correction, even though FP8 exceeds INT8 on absolute clean and corrupted AP in every matched block mean. In RetinaNet, a matched-recipe decomposition localizes a severe INT8 level deficit ($-\nnMatchedCleanDeficitKitti$ AP on KITTI) to the regression head: leaving that head in floating point restores clean-competitive accuracy without retraining, and a post-hoc operand factorial attributes essentially all of the deficit to activation quantization (weights contribute at most a $\sim$0.2-AP residual), while the same protocol on anchor-free FCOS shows no head-specific deficit. Corruption-aware calibration on two frozen folds transfers inconsistently, with a significant harm in one held-out cell. Robustness should be reported per recipe and per model region.
